LangLeap - Language Learning App for Hindi and Marathi Speakers

Problem Statement:
An edtech company wants an app that teaches English to Hindi and Marathi speakers through short
daily lessons, speaking practice and quizzes. Content writers, voice artists and developers must
work together, and content is the slowest part. The product head wants 120 lessons at launch; the
content team has written 35 in 2 months. The board has approved ₹35 lakh and 6 months. 

Given Project Data (use these figures - do not invent your own):
● Content rate: 35 lessons in 8 weeks. Compute the weekly rate and the weeks needed for 120 lessons.
● Voice recording: 1.5 hours per lesson; one voice artist at 15 hours per week. Compute weeks for 120 lessons.
● App development: 20 weeks. Identify which stream controls the launch. 

Objectives
● Requirements Engineering: Elicit requirements from learners, content writers, voice artists,
product head through interviews and analysis of the existing forms and spreadsheets; write 11
functional and 6 non-functional requirements covering offline behaviour, usability and content
quality, attach a measurable target to every NFR, and prioritise with MoSCoW.
● Software Design & UML: Produce use case, class, sequence (for 'complete a lesson and
unlock the next') and activity diagrams, plus a state diagram for the lesson progress; explain
how you kept content pipeline and app loosely coupled and each module cohesive.
● Testing & Quality Assurance: Design boundary value tests for the quiz score threshold
field, equivalence classes and a decision table for lesson unlock and streak rules, and compute
defect density and defect removal efficiency (DRE) from your test log.
● Scheduling & Milestones: Identify the controlling stream and build a Gantt chart with all
three streams. Show the forward pass, name the critical path, give the float of every
non-critical task, and draw the Gantt chart with milestones as zero-duration checkpoints.
● Estimation: Compute content and voice durations. Show every formula and intermediate
step, state your assumptions, and explain why the result is an estimate and not a promise.
● Risk Management: Build a risk register including content quality and artist availability.
Score each risk as probability x impact, rank by exposure, give each a response and an owner,
and keep problems that have already occurred in a separate issue log.


Outcomes
● A complete IEEE 830-style SRS in which every NFR (offline behaviour, usability and content
quality) is measurable - no 'the system shall be fast'.
● A UML package whose use case, class, sequence and state diagrams are consistent with one
another, with a written cohesion and coupling justification.
● Content and voice-recording durations calculated from actual rates.
● An analysis that identifies which of the three streams controls the launch.
● Systematically derived test cases, with DRE and defect-density figures, that would catch
errors in lesson unlock and streak rules.

Deliverables
● SRS Document - Scope, FRs, measurable NFRs, constraints, MoSCoW priorities and a
traceability matrix linking every FR to a test case.
● UML Package - Use case, class, sequence ('complete a lesson and unlock the next'), activity
and state (lesson progress) diagrams, internally consistent.
● Project Plan - WBS, network diagram with critical path, Gantt chart with milestones and
resource allocation.
● Estimation Sheet - Content and voice calculations; every calculation shown step by step,
with formulas, assumptions and confidence level.
● Test Plan & Evidence - Test cases, BVA and decision tables for lesson unlock and streak
rules, defect log, DRE and defect-density calculation.
● Risk Register & Closure Note - Probability-impact matrix, exposure-ranked risks with
RMMM for the top three, and a lessons-learned page.